export const BLOGS = [
  {
    id: "compress-images-website-speed", toolId: "compress",
    title: "Why Your Website Is Slow — And How Image Compression Fixes It",
    date: "March 15, 2026", author: "James Whitfield", readTime: "6 min read", cat: "Optimize",
    excerpt: "A 5-second page load costs you 40% of your visitors before they even see your content. The culprit is almost always the same thing: uncompressed images.",
    sections: [
      { h: "The invisible weight dragging your site down", b: `Most people obsess over hosting plans, CDNs, and caching plugins when their site runs slow. They ignore the most obvious fix sitting right in front of them: the images. A single high-resolution photo straight off a camera or stock site can weigh 8–12 MB. Put four of those on a homepage and you have a page that takes 10 seconds to load on a decent connection.\n\nGoogle's own data shows that 53% of mobile users abandon a page that takes more than three seconds to load. That's more than half your audience gone before they read a single word. And it's not just user experience — Google uses page speed as a ranking signal. A slow site ranks lower, which means less traffic and less revenue. It all traces back to those bloated images.` },
      { h: "What image compression actually does", b: `Compression reduces the amount of data stored in an image file. There are two types: lossless and lossy. Lossless removes redundant data without touching image quality — you get a smaller file that looks identical to the original. Lossy compression goes further, selectively discarding detail the human eye doesn't easily notice, getting you significantly smaller files in exchange for a tiny, usually invisible quality reduction.\n\nFor most web use cases — blog posts, product photos, landing pages — lossy compression at 70–80% quality is completely indistinguishable from the original. The difference in file size is dramatic. A 4 MB photo compressed to 70% typically comes out at 300–500 KB. That's an 85–90% reduction. Multiply that across a page with ten images and you've just removed roughly 35 MB of data your visitors had to download.` },
      { h: "Real results: what happens after you compress", b: `Images account for an average of 64% of a webpage's total size. After running a proper compression pass on a typical e-commerce site, page load times routinely drop from 6–8 seconds to under 2 seconds. That kind of improvement translates directly into conversions. Research consistently shows that every one-second improvement in mobile page load time increases conversion rates by up to 27%.\n\nIf your site converts at 2% with a 6-second load time, and compression gets you to 2-second loads, you could reasonably expect conversions to climb to 2.5% or higher. On any meaningful traffic volume, that difference adds up very fast.` },
      { h: "How to compress images without losing quality", b: `The key is finding the right quality setting for each image. A photograph with lots of colour gradients handles compression well — you can push it to 60% quality and it will still look sharp. A flat-colour graphic or a screenshot with text needs more careful handling, usually 80–90% to preserve readability.\n\nThe best workflow is to compress in bulk before uploading anything to your website. Use Pixalyse to drag in your images, set the quality slider, and download the compressed versions in seconds — no software, no plugin, no API key required. For an existing site, go through your media library monthly and compress anything over 500 KB.` },
      { h: "What format should you use after compressing?", b: `JPEG works best for photographs and complex images with lots of colours and gradients. PNG is better for images with transparency or flat colours — logos, icons, screenshots. WebP is the modern option that outperforms both in file size while maintaining quality, though older browsers don't always support it.\n\nFor most websites, JPEG at 75–80% quality is the sweet spot. Run it through a compressor, check the output looks good, and upload. Your visitors, your bounce rate, and your Google ranking will all thank you.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "resize-images-every-platform", toolId: "resize",
    title: "The Right Image Dimensions for Every Platform in 2026",
    date: "March 12, 2026", author: "Leila Nasser", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Edit",
    excerpt: "Upload the wrong size image and every platform will either crop it badly, stretch it, or compress it into a blurry mess. Here are the exact dimensions you need.",
    sections: [
      { h: "Why image dimensions matter more than you think", b: `Every platform — Instagram, Facebook, Twitter, LinkedIn, YouTube — has its own preferred image dimensions, and they're all different. Upload a 4000×3000 pixel photo to Instagram and it doesn't just scale it down nicely. It crops it according to its own rules, often cutting out exactly the part of the image you wanted to highlight.\n\nGetting image dimensions right isn't perfectionism. It's making sure your images display the way you intend them to, on every device, every time.` },
      { h: "Social media dimensions in 2026", b: `Instagram feed posts work best at 1080×1080 pixels for squares, 1080×1350 for portrait, and 1080×566 for landscape. Stories and Reels should be 1080×1920. Facebook cover photos are 820×312 on desktop. Twitter (X) header images are 1500×500. LinkedIn cover photos are 1128×191. YouTube thumbnails are 1280×720 — always design at 16:9 ratio. Pinterest pins perform best at 1000×1500 (2:3 ratio).` },
      { h: "Website and email image sizes", b: `For websites, hero images should generally be 1920×1080 pixels or wider. Blog post featured images work at 1200×630 — this also doubles as the Open Graph size that appears when someone shares your link on social media. Product images on e-commerce sites should be at least 800×800 for zoom functionality.\n\nEmail images should be designed at 600px wide for headers. Avoid anything wider than 650px or it will be clipped on mobile. Always test how images look at 300px wide since that approximates the mobile experience.` },
      { h: "How to resize without distortion", b: `The most important thing when resizing is maintaining the aspect ratio. If your original image is 1200×900 and you resize the width to 600, the height should automatically become 450 to maintain the same proportions. Forcing it into a different height will squash or stretch the image, which looks immediately wrong to anyone who sees it.\n\nPixalyse handles this automatically — enter the width or height you want, leave the other blank, and the correct matching dimension is calculated for you.` },
      { h: "Batch resizing for efficiency", b: `If you're managing a content calendar, you often need the same image in multiple sizes — one for Instagram, one for Twitter, one for your newsletter. Create a simple checklist of sizes you need for each platform and run through them in one session. With a fast browser-based tool, resizing a single image to five different dimensions takes about two minutes.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "png-vs-jpg-which-format", toolId: "png-to-jpg",
    title: "PNG vs JPG: Which Image Format Should You Actually Use?",
    date: "February 28, 2026", author: "Sara Okonkwo", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Convert",
    excerpt: "Most people just use whatever format their camera or app exports. But choosing the right format can cut your file sizes in half or prevent quality loss entirely.",
    sections: [
      { h: "The fundamental difference between PNG and JPG", b: `JPG uses lossy compression. When you save a JPG, it permanently discards some image data to make the file smaller. At high quality settings, the loss is essentially invisible. At low settings, you get blocky compression artefacts around edges and areas of sharp contrast.\n\nPNG uses lossless compression. Nothing is thrown away. Every pixel is stored exactly as it was captured or created. PNG files are larger than JPG files of the same image, but they're bit-perfect — no compression artefacts, no quality degradation, and critically, they support transparency (an alpha channel), which JPG does not.` },
      { h: "Use JPG for photographs", b: `Photographs are the natural home of JPG. They have millions of slightly different colour values, gradients, and complex detail. A photograph saved as PNG might be 8–15 MB. The same photograph saved as a high-quality JPG might be 1–3 MB. For web use, that difference matters enormously for page load times.\n\nAt quality settings of 80% or above, JPG is essentially indistinguishable from the lossless original for photographic content.` },
      { h: "Use PNG for graphics, logos, and screenshots", b: `PNG shines for images with flat colours, sharp edges, text, and transparency. A company logo, a screenshot of a user interface, an icon set — these need PNG. JPG compression creates visible artefacts around sharp colour transitions: the blocky, hazy quality you sometimes see around text in a JPG screenshot.\n\nTransparency is the other decisive factor. If you need your image to have a transparent background so it sits cleanly over any colour without a white box around it — JPG cannot do this at all. PNG was built for this use case.` },
      { h: "Converting between formats: when and why", b: `Converting a JPG to PNG doesn't recover any of the quality that was lost in the original JPG compression. What it does is prevent further quality loss. If you need to edit and re-save a file multiple times, do your editing in PNG to avoid the cumulative quality degradation of multiple rounds of JPG compression, then export as JPG at the end.\n\nConverting a PNG to JPG makes sense when you have a photograph that was saved as PNG for no good reason. If the image is a photograph with no transparent areas, converting to JPG at 80% quality produces a file that's 60–80% smaller with no perceptible quality loss.` },
      { h: "The modern alternative: WebP", b: `WebP achieves 25–35% smaller files than JPG at equivalent quality, and beats PNG on file size while maintaining lossless quality for graphics with transparency. Browser support is now essentially universal.\n\nIf you're building for the web and have control over your image pipeline, WebP is worth using. If you're working with images for general use, print, or platforms that might not support WebP, JPG and PNG remain the reliable standards.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "heic-to-jpg-iphone-problem-explained", toolId: "heic-to-jpg",
    title: "Why Your iPhone Photos Won't Open on Windows (The HEIC Problem Explained)",
    date: "February 25, 2026", author: "James Whitfield", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Convert",
    excerpt: "Since iOS 11, iPhones save photos in HEIC format by default. It's 40% smaller than JPG — but won't open on most Windows computers or older software.",
    sections: [
      { h: "What is HEIC and why Apple switched to it", b: `HEIC stands for High Efficiency Image Container. It's based on the HEVC video compression standard and produces files roughly 40–50% smaller than equivalent JPG files while maintaining the same or better image quality. Apple adopted it starting with iOS 11 in 2017, and for good reason: for a phone with limited storage that takes hundreds of photos, this is a meaningful difference.\n\nHEIC also supports 16-bit colour depth, HDR images, and image sequences — features that JPG doesn't support at all. From a technical standpoint, it's a genuinely superior format for modern photography.` },
      { h: "The compatibility problem", b: `The problem is that HEIC is an Apple format in a world that still runs mostly on JPG. Windows 10 and 11 don't open HEIC files by default — you either get an error or need to install the Microsoft HEVC Video Extensions codec. Android phones generally can't open HEIC files from iPhone. Most image editing software, web upload forms, and content management systems don't accept HEIC.\n\nWhen you try to share iPhone photos with someone who doesn't use Apple devices, you often end up in a frustrating cycle of failed attempts and requests to resend.` },
      { h: "How to change iPhone settings to shoot in JPG instead", b: `Go to Settings → Camera → Formats and switch from "High Efficiency" to "Most Compatible". Your photos will now save as JPG, which opens everywhere. The tradeoff is that your photos will take up roughly twice as much storage space. On an iPhone with 64GB or less this might matter, but on larger-capacity phones it generally won't.` },
      { h: "When you already have HEIC files and need to convert", b: `If you have a library of HEIC photos you need to share, edit, or upload to a website, you need to convert them. On a Mac, Preview can open HEIC files natively and export as JPG. On Windows, it's more complicated without the right codec installed.\n\nThe simplest cross-platform solution is a browser-based converter. Upload the HEIC file, get a JPG back, done. No software installation, no codec downloads, works on any operating system. Pixalyse accepts HEIC files and converts them to JPG in seconds.` },
      { h: "Does converting HEIC to JPG lose quality?", b: `Converting from HEIC to JPG at 80%+ quality settings produces a result that's visually indistinguishable from the original. HEIC's technical superiority doesn't matter for standard photo sharing and printing. What you lose in conversion is the file size efficiency — a converted JPG will be larger than the HEIC original. But you gain universal compatibility, which is almost always worth it.\n\nIf you're archiving photos and have the storage space, keeping the HEIC originals alongside converted JPGs is the ideal setup: HEIC for archival fidelity, JPG for sharing and compatibility.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "batch-compress-images-guide", toolId: "bulk-compress",
    title: "How to Compress 100 Images in Under a Minute",
    date: "February 12, 2026", author: "Leila Nasser", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Optimize",
    excerpt: "Compressing images one by one is one of the most tedious tasks in digital content work. Here's how to do it in bulk, efficiently, without losing quality control.",
    sections: [
      { h: "When you need to compress in bulk", b: `Batch compression needs are everywhere. You've just received 200 product images from a photographer, all shot at full camera resolution — 20–30 MB each, completely impractical for web use. You have a folder of 500 marketing images accumulated over the past year. You're preparing an image-heavy email campaign and need all assets under 500 KB each.\n\nDoing this one at a time isn't just slow — it's the kind of repetitive, low-value work that eats hours that could be spent on something useful. Batch compression solves this by applying the same quality settings to every file in one operation.` },
      { h: "Choosing the right quality setting", b: `Different types of images tolerate different quality levels. Photographs look fine at 70–80% quality. Flat-colour graphics and screenshots start showing compression artefacts at 80% and need 85–90% to stay clean. Text-heavy images need 90%+ to remain readable.\n\nFor a mixed batch — the typical case — 80% is a reasonable universal setting that's safe for photographs and acceptable for most graphics. If your batch is all photographs, go lower (70%). If it's all graphics, go higher (85–90%). Spot-check 10–15% of the output to verify quality without reviewing every file.` },
      { h: "File naming and organisation", b: `When batch-compressing, output file naming is important. You want to be able to tell which files have been compressed and which haven't, and you want the compressed files to replace the originals in your workflow without confusion.\n\nCommon approaches: add a suffix (_compressed, _web) to the output filename so the compressed version is clearly identified. Or process into a separate folder — original/ → compressed/ — and use the compressed versions going forward while keeping originals as backup. Never overwrite the originals until you've verified the compressed versions are acceptable.` },
      { h: "Where to use batch-compressed images", b: `Website uploads are the primary use case. Before anything goes into your CMS or website builder, it should be compressed. This is especially important for e-commerce sites where product images dominate the page weight. Email campaigns benefit enormously — smaller images load faster and clear attachment size limits. Internal document sharing also benefits: a 50 MB PowerPoint of uncompressed photos becomes 10 MB after compression.` },
      { h: "The ongoing compression habit", b: `The most effective approach isn't a one-time cleanup but an ongoing habit. Every image that enters your workflow — screenshots, downloads, photos from cameras or phones, graphics from design tools — gets compressed before use. This keeps file sizes under control at the source rather than requiring periodic cleanups.\n\nWith a fast browser-based tool, a batch of images processes in seconds. Building this step into your workflow takes five minutes to set up and saves hours of cleanup later.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "upscale-images-ai-explained", toolId: "upscale",
    title: "Can You Actually Make a Blurry Photo Sharp Again? The Truth About AI Upscaling",
    date: "March 4, 2026", author: "Leila Nasser", readTime: "6 min read", cat: "AI Tools",
    excerpt: "AI upscaling has gotten genuinely impressive — but it's not magic. Here's what it can and can't do, and the situations where it makes a real difference.",
    sections: [
      { h: "What AI upscaling actually does", b: `Traditional image upscaling works by interpolation — it looks at existing pixels and creates new ones by averaging values between them. The result is a larger image that's almost always blurry. AI upscaling uses machine learning models trained on millions of image pairs to learn what high-resolution details typically look like for given types of content.\n\nWhen it upscales a face, it doesn't just average nearby pixels — it recognises that this is a face and uses its training to reconstruct likely detail in eyes, skin texture, and edges. The results aren't perfect, but they're often dramatically better than traditional interpolation.` },
      { h: "When AI upscaling makes a real difference", b: `Old photos are the killer application. Family photos from the 1980s and 90s, scanned at low resolution, often have enough visual information for AI to reconstruct meaningful detail. Faces sharpen up considerably, textures become more defined, and the image looks substantially more like something you could print and frame.\n\nLow-resolution product images are another strong use case. If you have product photos shot at 400×400 pixels that you need to display at 800×800, AI upscaling gives you a far better result than just doubling the pixels. The product will look sharper, edges will be cleaner, and details will be more legible.` },
      { h: "What upscaling cannot fix", b: `If the original image is motion-blurred — the subject moved while the shutter was open — AI upscaling can't reverse that blur. It can make the image larger, but the smeared detail is baked into the pixels and no amount of AI inference can reconstruct what was never captured. The same applies to out-of-focus blur.\n\nHeavy JPEG compression artefacts can be partially reduced by AI upscaling, but if the detail was destroyed in compression, reconstruction is estimation rather than recovery. The AI's guesses are often better-looking than the artefacts they replace, but they're still guesses.` },
      { h: "2x vs 4x: how much should you upscale?", b: `The more you upscale, the more the AI has to invent. A 2x upscale of a moderately sharp 800×600 image typically looks good — there's enough original detail for the model to work with. A 4x upscale of a tiny, blurry 200×150 thumbnail asks the AI to essentially draw most of the image from very little information.\n\nAs a rule of thumb: 2x is almost always safe and produces excellent results. 4x works well on images with clear structure — faces, text, products with defined edges — but can look over-processed on complex scenes. Try 2x first; only push to 4x if you need more.` },
      { h: "Practical applications beyond photography", b: `Old video game screenshots, retro pixel art displayed at large sizes, low-resolution icons, and historical document scans all benefit from upscaling. Web developers use it to prepare images for high-DPI (Retina) displays, where a physical screen has twice the pixel density of a standard display — a 2x upscaled image fills those extra pixels with sharper detail.\n\nThe key is setting realistic expectations. AI upscaling consistently makes images look better and larger. Whether the result is good enough for your specific purpose depends on the quality of the original and the standards of your output medium.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "how-to-watermark-photos", toolId: "watermark",
    title: "How to Watermark Your Photos and Actually Protect Your Work",
    date: "March 6, 2026", author: "James Whitfield", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Edit",
    excerpt: "Your photos are being used without permission right now. Here's how watermarks work, what actually deters theft, and how to do it without ruining the image.",
    sections: [
      { h: "The reality of image theft online", b: `If you post images publicly online — whether you're a photographer, designer, blogger, or brand — your images are being copied without your permission. This isn't paranoia; it's the default behaviour of the internet. Watermarks don't make images impossible to steal. What they do is ensure that when an image gets shared, reposted, or used without permission, your name or logo travels with it.\n\nThat's a different value proposition than prevention — it's attribution and passive marketing. Every person who sees your watermarked image on someone else's platform is a potential visitor to your site.` },
      { h: "Where to place your watermark", b: `A watermark in the dead centre is the most protection against removal — it covers the main subject and can't be cropped out. But it also ruins the image for legitimate viewers, which defeats the purpose of sharing it.\n\nThe most practical placements are the lower-right corner (the viewer's natural exit point after reading an image) or the lower-left. These are visible but not intrusive. The lower-right corner is standard for photo credits and is widely understood as such, making it feel less aggressive than a centred overlay.` },
      { h: "Opacity: the balance between visible and intrusive", b: `A watermark at full opacity is annoying. A watermark at 10% opacity is invisible and defeats the purpose. The sweet spot for most images is 30–50% opacity — visible enough to be readable on close inspection, but not so heavy that it dominates the image.\n\nTest your watermark on images with different backgrounds. A white watermark at 40% opacity reads clearly on dark backgrounds but disappears on light ones. Consider adding a dark shadow or outline effect if your images have unpredictable backgrounds.` },
      { h: "What to use as your watermark", b: `The most effective watermarks are either your name (for personal brand) or your website URL (for traffic generation). A website URL is arguably better — not only does it credit you, it actively drives people who see the watermarked image back to your site.\n\nKeep watermark text short and readable. Your name, business name, or clean URL. Avoid long phrases or slogans that become visually noisy at the opacity levels required for non-intrusive watermarks.` },
      { h: "Batch watermarking for high-volume publishing", b: `If you're a photographer or content creator publishing regularly, watermarking individual images is unsustainable. The solution is to build watermarking into your export workflow. Before any image goes onto your website or social accounts, it goes through a quick watermark pass. With Pixalyse you can set your text, position, opacity, and colour once and process images in seconds. Keep the same settings every time so your watermarks look uniform and professional across your body of work.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "blur-background-portrait-photography", toolId: "blur-bg",
    title: "How to Get a Blurred Background Without an Expensive Camera",
    date: "March 2, 2026", author: "Tom Everett", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "AI Tools",
    excerpt: "That creamy blurred background in professional portraits is called bokeh. You don't need a DSLR to get it — here's what creates it and how to fake it convincingly.",
    sections: [
      { h: "Why blurred backgrounds look professional", b: `The blurred background effect — technically called bokeh — does one important thing: it separates the subject from the background. When everything in a photo is in equal focus, the viewer's eye doesn't know where to look. The background competes with the subject. When the background is blurred, the subject is isolated visually and the eye goes there immediately.\n\nThis is why portraits taken with expensive cameras and wide-aperture lenses look so much more professional than phone photos in front of the same background. It's not just resolution or colour rendering — it's the depth-of-field effect that tells the viewer exactly where to focus.` },
      { h: "How your phone's portrait mode works", b: `Modern smartphones have portrait mode, which simulates this effect using software. The phone takes the image, detects the subject using AI, creates a mask separating subject from background, and applies blur to everything outside the mask.\n\nPortrait mode struggles with hair, glasses, and complex backgrounds. You'll often see the telltale halo effect around hair, or glasses that are partially blurred when they shouldn't be. The technology has improved dramatically but still has consistent failure modes on difficult subjects.` },
      { h: "When to add blur in post-processing", b: `Sometimes you didn't shoot with portrait mode, or the portrait mode result had errors, or you're working with a photo that wasn't taken as a portrait at all. Adding background blur in post-processing is a legitimate and effective technique.\n\nThe quality of the result depends on the contrast between subject and background. Clean edges — a person against a solid-colour wall — produce convincing blurs. Complex scenes with many overlapping elements are harder to separate cleanly and the blur effect will have more visible errors.` },
      { h: "How much blur is appropriate?", b: `Over-blurred backgrounds look fake and processed. The goal is to reduce the background's visual competition, not make it a foggy mess. In professional photography, standard lens bokeh is significant but not extreme — details are soft but shapes and colours are still identifiable.\n\nWhen applying blur digitally, start at a low value and increase gradually. For a typical portrait, 8–12 pixels of blur on a web-sized image reads as natural. More than 20 pixels starts to look obviously artificial.` },
      { h: "Photography habits that help", b: `If you regularly shoot content on a phone and want more professional-looking results, a few habits will help even before post-processing. Get closer to your subject — the closer the camera, the more natural depth separation exists. Create distance between the subject and the background — stand your subject a metre or two from the wall rather than right against it. Use the longest zoom on your phone, which compresses depth and increases background blur naturally. Combined with background blur in post-processing, these habits produce genuinely professional results without camera upgrades.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "svg-to-png-designers-guide", toolId: "svg-to-png",
    title: "SVG vs PNG: A Designer's Guide to Choosing the Right Vector Export",
    date: "February 22, 2026", author: "Leila Nasser", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Convert",
    excerpt: "SVG is perfect for logos and icons — until it isn't. Here's when you need to convert to PNG, how to do it without quality loss, and the scale settings that matter.",
    sections: [
      { h: "What makes SVG special", b: `SVG stands for Scalable Vector Graphics. Unlike PNG, JPG, and other raster formats, SVG doesn't store images as a grid of pixels. It stores mathematical descriptions of shapes — circles, paths, curves, fills, and gradients. Because the image is described mathematically, it can be rendered at any size without any loss of quality. A logo saved as SVG looks identically sharp at 100×100 pixels and at 10,000×10,000 pixels.\n\nThis makes SVG the standard format for logos, icons, illustrations, and any graphic that needs to be displayed at multiple sizes.` },
      { h: "When SVG isn't the right choice", b: `SVG doesn't work for photographs or complex images with millions of colours. But even for graphics that are native SVGs, there are situations where PNG is necessary. Many platforms don't accept SVG uploads — Facebook, Instagram, Twitter, most content management systems, and email clients won't take SVG files. Print services often need raster files with specific pixel dimensions. In all these cases, you need to convert SVG to PNG.` },
      { h: "Scale matters: export at the right size", b: `This is where most people make the critical mistake. They convert their SVG to PNG without thinking about the output size, and they end up with a 100×100 pixel PNG from a logo that needs to be displayed at 400×400. The result is blurry, and the SVG's scalability advantage is entirely lost.\n\nWhen converting SVG to PNG, export at the largest size you'll ever need, then scale down from there. Scaling down always looks better than scaling up. For logos that might be used for print, export at 2× or 3× the intended display size to ensure sharpness on high-DPI screens.` },
      { h: "Preserving transparency in PNG exports", b: `One of SVG's features is that backgrounds are transparent by default — your logo or icon sits cleanly over any background without a white box. When converting to PNG, you want to preserve this transparency rather than adding a white background.\n\nPNG supports transparency natively, so a properly executed SVG-to-PNG conversion maintains the transparent background. Always test the exported PNG by placing it over a dark background to verify the transparency is intact — some tools add a white background without warning.` },
      { h: "Keep the SVG as your master file", b: `The ideal workflow is to keep your SVG as the master file and export PNG versions as needed. The SVG is always scalable, always editable, always perfect. Name PNG exports descriptively: logo_400x200.png, logo_1000x500.png. When a new size is needed, generate it fresh from the SVG rather than scaling an existing PNG. This ensures consistent sharpness regardless of the output size required.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "how-to-crop-images-like-pro", toolId: "crop",
    title: "How to Crop Images Like a Professional (Without Photoshop)",
    date: "March 10, 2026", author: "Tom Everett", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Edit",
    excerpt: "Cropping isn't just cutting things out — it's the most powerful composition tool you have after the photo is taken. Here's how to use it properly.",
    sections: [
      { h: "Cropping is a creative decision, not a technical one", b: `Most people crop images to remove something — a bin in the corner, a tourist in the background, a distracting element on the edge. That's valid, but it's only half of what cropping can do. Professional photographers use cropping as a deliberate composition choice. The same photo, cropped three different ways, tells three different stories.\n\nA wide crop showing a person in their environment gives context and scale. A tight crop focusing on their face gives intimacy and emotion. A square crop centres the subject symmetrically. Learning to see these possibilities is one of the most valuable image editing skills there is.` },
      { h: "The rule of thirds", b: `Divide your image into a 3×3 grid — three columns and three rows, creating nine equal rectangles. The rule of thirds says that the most visually interesting compositions place the key subject at one of the four intersection points of these grid lines, not dead centre. Centred subjects feel static and formal. Off-centre subjects feel natural and dynamic.\n\nWhen you crop a photo, try to position the main subject at one of these intersection points. For a portrait, put one eye at an intersection. For a landscape, put the horizon on either the top or bottom third line, not the middle.` },
      { h: "Aspect ratios for different purposes", b: `Different contexts demand different aspect ratios, and cropping is how you get from your original image to the ratio you need. Square (1:1) is Instagram's home territory and feels balanced and symmetrical. Portrait (4:5 or 2:3) is tall and works well for phone screens. Landscape (16:9) is the standard for video thumbnails, presentations, and widescreen monitors.\n\nThink about where your cropped image will live before you crop it. If it's going on Instagram, crop to 1080×1080 or 1080×1350. If it's a YouTube thumbnail, crop to 16:9 first.` },
      { h: "What to remove and what to keep", b: `The question to ask before every crop is: what does this image need to communicate? Everything that answers that question stays. Everything that doesn't goes. Be ruthless with negative space. Empty sky, blank walls, and dead corners that don't contribute to the story should go.\n\nBut be careful not to crop too tight — leaving a little breathing room around the subject usually feels more comfortable than pressing right up against it.` },
      { h: "Cropping for text overlays", b: `If your image is destined to have text overlaid — a social media post, a blog header, an advertisement — you need to crop with that text in mind. Leave clear space where the text will sit. Avoid placing text over complex, busy areas of the image where it will be hard to read.\n\nA good approach is to crop so there's a large area of relatively flat colour or blur where the text will go, with the interesting part of the image on the opposite side. This gives you a readable overlay and a visually interesting composition at the same time.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "when-rotate-flip-images", toolId: "rotate",
    title: "When and Why You Should Rotate or Flip Your Images",
    date: "March 8, 2026", author: "Sara Okonkwo", readTime: "4 min read", cat: "Edit",
    excerpt: "Phone cameras auto-rotate images based on how you held the device. Sometimes they get it wrong. Here's how to fix it — and when flipping can actually improve a photo.",
    sections: [
      { h: "The EXIF rotation problem", b: `You take a photo holding your phone in portrait orientation. The photo looks perfect on your phone. You upload it to your website and suddenly it's sideways. This happens because of EXIF metadata — data embedded in image files that tells software how to display the image. Your phone stores the rotation as a tag rather than actually rotating the pixel data.\n\nMost modern software reads this tag and rotates accordingly. But some applications don't read EXIF data, so they display the raw pixels without rotation. The fix is to actually rotate the pixel data and save the image without relying on the EXIF tag.` },
      { h: "Which rotation do you need?", b: `If your image was taken with the phone rotated clockwise, rotate 90 degrees counter-clockwise to correct it. If taken with the phone rotated counter-clockwise, rotate 90 degrees clockwise. If it's upside down, rotate 180 degrees.\n\nA quick way to figure it out: look at the image and ask which way you'd physically turn the screen to make it right. If you'd turn the screen 90 degrees clockwise, the fix is to rotate the image 90 degrees counter-clockwise.` },
      { h: "Horizontal flipping: more useful than you think", b: `Flipping an image horizontally creates a mirror image. In design, you might want a person looking to the right instead of the left — a horizontal flip achieves this instantly. When creating a layout where two images face each other, flipping one creates visual symmetry and makes them appear to be in conversation.\n\nFor product photography, a flip can change which side of the product faces the viewer. It's often the fastest way to rebalance a layout when a new element has been added.` },
      { h: "Vertical flipping and when it makes sense", b: `Vertical flipping — creating an upside-down version — has its uses. Reflection effects in design often use a vertically flipped copy of an image placed below the original, with reduced opacity, to simulate a water or surface reflection. This is a classic technique for product shots, giving them a polished, high-end look without any additional photography.\n\nSome experimental designs use vertical flips for artistic effect — the disorientation of an upside-down familiar object can be deliberately arresting.` },
      { h: "Batch rotating scanned documents", b: `If you've been scanning documents and they've come out at the wrong orientation, rotating them one by one is tedious. The more efficient approach: scan everything first, then go through the batch and rotate as needed. Most scanning setups produce consistent errors — if the scanner rotated everything 90 degrees clockwise, every page needs the same correction. A fast browser-based tool saves a lot of time when dealing with large stacks of documents.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "images-to-pdf-easy-way", toolId: "image-to-pdf",
    title: "How to Combine Multiple Photos into One PDF File",
    date: "February 20, 2026", author: "Tom Everett", readTime: "4 min read", cat: "Convert",
    excerpt: "Sending 20 separate image files is annoying for everyone. A single PDF is professional, easy to open, and easy to print. Here's how to do it without any software.",
    sections: [
      { h: "Why PDF beats sending a folder of images", b: `When you have a collection of images to share — product photos, scanned documents, a portfolio — the instinct is to zip everything or attach files individually. Both create friction. A zip file requires extraction. Individual attachments require opening one by one. On mobile, viewing twenty separate images means navigating back and forth between files.\n\nA PDF changes the experience entirely. It's a single file that opens in one click on every device without installing anything. Pages are numbered. The viewer can scroll through everything in sequence. It looks professional.` },
      { h: "Common use cases", b: `Scanned documents are the most frequent use case. If you scan multiple pages of a contract or a form, you want one PDF, not fifteen separate JPEG files. Many identity verification platforms want a single PDF containing both sides of your ID, not two image files.\n\nPortfolios are another major use case. Creative professionals can combine their best images into a single PDF that's easy to forward and review. Event photographers can deliver a curated selection as a PDF that clients can review end-to-end.` },
      { h: "Image order matters — plan before you convert", b: `The order you select or upload your images is the order they'll appear in the PDF. Spend thirty seconds arranging the images in the sequence they should be read before converting.\n\nRenaming your files with a numerical prefix (01_image.jpg, 02_image.jpg) before uploading is the most reliable way to ensure correct order regardless of which tool processes them.` },
      { h: "Image quality in the final PDF", b: `PDF creation tools typically apply their own compression to images when embedding them. The result is a file smaller than the sum of the original images but where images are still readable and printable. For most purposes — sharing documents, submitting forms, presenting portfolios — this quality level is entirely adequate.` },
      { h: "File size considerations", b: `A PDF containing twenty high-resolution photographs can easily exceed 50 MB — creating problems for email attachments and upload limits. If file size matters, compress your images before converting them to PDF. Bringing each image from 3 MB to 500 KB before combining can take you from a 60 MB file to a 10 MB file while maintaining quality adequate for screen and standard printing.\n\nThe workflow: compress images first, arrange in order, then convert to PDF.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "pdf-to-image-extract-pages", toolId: "pdf-to-image",
    title: "How to Extract Images from a PDF Without Any Software",
    date: "February 18, 2026", author: "Sara Okonkwo", readTime: "4 min read", cat: "Convert",
    excerpt: "Whether you need a single page as an image or every page extracted separately, there are faster ways to do this than Adobe Reader.",
    sections: [
      { h: "Why you'd need to convert PDF pages to images", b: `PDFs are wonderful for sharing but frustrating when you need to do anything other than read them. You can't post a PDF page on Instagram. You can't use a PDF as a website image. You can't edit individual pages in an image editor. Converting PDF pages to JPG or PNG images unlocks all of these possibilities.\n\nYou can take a presentation slide and post it as a social media image. Extract a chart from a report to use in a blog post. Share a readable image of one page from a contract without sending the whole document.` },
      { h: "Full page extraction vs extracting embedded images", b: `There are two different things people mean by extracting images from a PDF. The first is converting each page to an image — essentially a high-resolution screenshot of the page. The second is extracting images that are embedded within the PDF — pulling out the original files that were inserted when the PDF was created.\n\nPage-to-image conversion produces a faithful render of exactly what the page looks like, including text, layout, and images. For most purposes — sharing, posting, editing — page-to-image conversion is what you want.` },
      { h: "Resolution matters: how to get sharp results", b: `A PDF page rendered at 72 DPI produces a blurry, unreadable image. At 150 DPI you get something acceptable for screen use. At 300 DPI you get a result that's print-quality sharp.\n\nMost professional PDF-to-image tools let you specify the output DPI. When using Pixalyse, the scale setting effectively controls resolution — a 2x scale renders the page at twice the standard pixel density, giving much sharper results without any settings to configure.` },
      { h: "Handling multi-page PDFs", b: `A multi-page PDF requires exporting each page as a separate image file. A 10-page document becomes 10 separate JPG files. Most tools handle this automatically, producing numbered output files that you can use individually or recombine.\n\nIf you only need specific pages rather than all of them, look for tools that let you specify a page range — this avoids generating and downloading images for pages you don't need.` },
      { h: "Common scenarios", b: `Sharing a presentation slide: convert the PDF to images, pick the relevant page, post directly. Creating a thumbnail for a PDF document: convert the first page to an image and use it as the preview thumbnail on your website. Editing a PDF page: convert it to an image, open in any image editor, make your changes, re-export. Extracting a signature from a scanned PDF: convert the relevant page to PNG, then use cropping to isolate the signature.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "image-base64-developers-guide", toolId: "base64",
    title: "What is Base64 and Why Do Developers Encode Images This Way?",
    date: "February 10, 2026", author: "Tom Everett", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Developer",
    excerpt: "Base64 is one of those things developers use constantly but rarely explain. Here's what it actually is, why it exists, and when to use it for images.",
    sections: [
      { h: "What Base64 encoding actually does", b: `Binary data — the kind that makes up image files, audio files, and other non-text content — can contain any combination of bytes, including bytes that have special meanings in text contexts. If you try to send raw binary data through a system designed for text (like email, HTML, or JSON), those special bytes can corrupt or break the data.\n\nBase64 encoding converts binary data into a string of printable ASCII characters using only 64 safe characters (A–Z, a–z, 0–9, +, /). The result is a text string that can pass safely through any text-based system. The tradeoff: Base64-encoded data is about 33% larger than the original binary.` },
      { h: "How Base64 images are used in web development", b: `The most common web use case is embedding images directly in HTML or CSS without a separate HTTP request. Instead of referencing an image file by its path, you embed the full Base64 string directly in the HTML or CSS. This eliminates the network request needed to load the image.\n\nFor small images — icons, thumbnails, inline decorative elements — this can improve page load performance by reducing round-trips to the server. The same technique works in CSS for background images.` },
      { h: "When to use Base64 and when not to", b: `Base64 is appropriate for small images — under 5–10 KB. A 2 KB icon embedded as Base64 saves a network request and adds only about 2.7 KB to your HTML. For larger images, the calculation inverts. A 100 KB photo becomes 133 KB of data embedded in your HTML that cannot be cached — re-downloaded every page load.\n\nDon't use Base64 for: hero images, large product photos, any image the user encounters on multiple pages where browser caching would be beneficial, or any image over about 10 KB.` },
      { h: "API and data transfer use cases", b: `Beyond web embedding, Base64 is widely used when sending images through APIs. Many image recognition APIs, AI APIs, and document processing APIs accept images as Base64 strings in JSON payloads rather than as file uploads.\n\nIf you're integrating with an API that requires Base64-encoded images, Pixalyse's Image to Base64 tool produces the complete data URI string — including the data:image/jpeg;base64 prefix — ready to use directly in code or API calls.` },
      { h: "Decoding Base64 images", b: `Going the other direction — converting a Base64 string back to an image file — is equally important to understand. Most programming languages have built-in Base64 decode functions. In JavaScript, atob() gives you the raw binary string which you can then convert to a Blob and download.\n\nWhen you receive a Base64-encoded image from an API response or database, you need to decode it before displaying or saving it. Understanding both encoding and decoding is fundamental to working with image data in any web or API context.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "passport-photo-at-home-guide", toolId: "passport",
    title: "How to Take Your Own Passport Photo at Home That Gets Accepted",
    date: "February 8, 2026", author: "Sara Okonkwo", readTime: "6 min read", cat: "Special",
    excerpt: "Passport photo booths charge £12–15 for photos that often get rejected anyway. A phone camera, good lighting, and the right background gets you the same result for free.",
    sections: [
      { h: "Why passport photos get rejected", b: `The most common rejection reasons: background isn't plain white or off-white (coloured walls, shadows on the wall), eyes are partially closed or obscured by glasses, head is tilted, facial expression includes a smile (many countries now require a neutral expression), and poor lighting creating shadows on the face or background.\n\nUnderstanding what gets photos rejected is more useful than memorising all the requirements, because most rejections come from a handful of consistent mistakes.` },
      { h: "The setup you need", b: `Background: Find a plain white or off-white wall. If you don't have one, tape white A3 paper to a wall and stand far enough from it that there's no shadow. Lighting: Natural light from a window directly in front of you gives flat, even light without harsh shadows. Camera: A smartphone on a flat surface or tripod at eye level works perfectly. Have someone take the photo rather than using a selfie — selfies introduce perspective distortion that makes faces look wider.` },
      { h: "Taking the photo: dos and don'ts", b: `Expression: look directly at the camera with a neutral expression — neither a full smile nor a frown, just relaxed. Most passport authorities now require that teeth are not showing. Glasses: most countries now require that glasses are removed — reflections, tinted lenses, and thick frames are common rejection reasons even where glasses aren't officially banned. Head position: face directly forward, head level, not tilted. No hats, no hair covering the face. Take twenty shots and pick the best.` },
      { h: "Getting the right dimensions", b: `Different countries have different required dimensions. US passports require 2×2 inch photos. UK, Indian, and Australian passports require 35×45mm. China requires 40×60mm. The specific dimensions are listed on your country's passport application page. The head must typically fill 70–80% of the frame height.\n\nPixalyse's Passport Photo tool handles the sizing standards for major countries automatically — select your country's standard and the output is correctly sized.` },
      { h: "Printing considerations", b: `Home-printed passport photos are accepted in most countries if print quality is adequate. Use a photo printer with photo paper, not a regular paper printer. The photo must not have a border, must be in colour unless your country specifically allows black and white, and must not be a digital image displayed on a screen.\n\nAlternatively, email your correctly sized photo to a print shop or pharmacy — they print at the right resolution on the right paper for a fraction of the booth price.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "screenshot-to-pdf-workflow", toolId: "screenshot-to-pdf",
    title: "The Fastest Way to Turn Screenshots into a Professional PDF Report",
    date: "February 5, 2026", author: "James Whitfield", readTime: "4 min read", cat: "Convert",
    excerpt: "Screenshots are the fastest way to capture visual information. Here's how to turn a messy folder of screenshots into a clean, shareable PDF.",
    sections: [
      { h: "When screenshots need to become documents", b: `Screenshots are uniquely useful for capturing things that exist only on screens — software interfaces, website content, chat conversations, error messages, data dashboards. But screenshots as individual files are disorganised and hard to share professionally.\n\nConverting to a PDF creates a structured, sequential document. This is essential for bug reports (each step documented and compiled), compliance documentation, client reporting (dashboard screenshots compiled monthly), and legal or evidentiary purposes (screenshots of conversations or transactions preserved in sequence).` },
      { h: "Naming your screenshots for correct ordering", b: `The single most important step is file naming. Screenshots taken in rapid succession are usually named with sequential numbers by your operating system. Problems arise when screenshots are taken at different times, renamed, or mixed with other files.\n\nBefore converting, rename everything with a consistent numerical prefix: 01_login_screen.png, 02_dashboard.png, 03_error_state.png. This ensures the PDF is assembled in exactly the order you intend, regardless of which tool processes them.` },
      { h: "Optimising screenshots before converting", b: `Raw screenshots from a Retina screen can be 3–8 MB each. A 20-screenshot PDF built from uncompressed files might be 100 MB — far too large to email or upload. Compressing the screenshots before conversion is worth the extra step.\n\nFor most purposes, compressing screenshots at 85–90% quality before conversion gives a good balance of file size and readability. If your screenshots contain small text that must be perfectly readable, stay at 90% or above.` },
      { h: "What makes a good screenshot PDF", b: `Pages should be in logical order that tells a clear story or follows a process. If the screenshots are documenting a problem, start with context (what the expected state should be) and progress to the error. If documenting a process, flow step-by-step.\n\nA PDF named Q1_Analytics_Report.pdf is immediately understood. A PDF named screenshots_2026.pdf tells the reader nothing. The filename itself is part of the professionalism.` },
      { h: "Use cases that work especially well", b: `Software testing and QA reports: every test case documented with screenshots, compiled chronologically. Client deliverable reports showing work completed, metrics achieved, or changes made. Training and onboarding documentation where each step of a process needs to be shown. Insurance and warranty claims where you need to demonstrate a defect or damage over time.\n\nIn all these cases, the PDF format makes the screenshot collection significantly more useful than a folder of individual image files.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "gifs-killing-page-speed", toolId: "gif-to-mp4",
    title: "GIFs Are Killing Your Page Speed — Here's What to Use Instead",
    date: "February 15, 2026", author: "James Whitfield", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Convert",
    excerpt: "A typical animated GIF is 5–10x larger than an equivalent MP4 video. If you're still using GIFs on your website, you're making visitors wait for no good reason.",
    sections: [
      { h: "The GIF format is ancient and inefficient", b: `GIF was created in 1987. It uses a compression algorithm that was state-of-the-art three and a half decades ago. Modern video compression has completely overtaken it. An animated GIF showing a five-second clip might weigh 8 MB. The same clip as an MP4 using H.264 compression weighs 500 KB. As a WebM file it might be 300 KB.\n\nThat's a 15–25x difference in file size for the exact same visual content. Every time a visitor loads a page with an animated GIF, their browser downloads all those megabytes.` },
      { h: "Why GIFs refuse to die", b: `Despite their inefficiency, GIFs have two things going for them. First, they're universally supported — every browser, every operating system, every email client, every messaging app. Second, they're easy to share — drop a GIF into a tweet, Slack message, or email and it just works, no video player or codec required.\n\nFor social media and messaging, GIFs are still practical because platforms often transcode them to video internally anyway. For websites you control, there's no good reason to use GIF.` },
      { h: "The HTML video solution for websites", b: `The correct modern approach for looping animations on websites is the HTML video element with autoplay, loop, muted, and playsinline attributes. The video plays automatically, loops endlessly, has no sound, and requires no user interaction — exactly like a GIF. But because it uses modern video compression, the file size is dramatically smaller and the playback quality is often better.\n\nOffering both MP4 and WebM versions covers all browsers. WebM provides the smallest file sizes on browsers that support it; MP4 is the fallback for everything else.` },
      { h: "GIF to MP4 conversion", b: `Converting an existing GIF to MP4 requires either server-side processing (FFmpeg is the standard tool) or a web service. The conversion process: FFmpeg reads the GIF frame by frame and re-encodes using H.264 compression. Quality, frame rate, and resolution can all be preserved or adjusted. The result looks identical to the GIF at a fraction of the file size.\n\nFor a high-traffic website where performance matters, converting every GIF to MP4 and replacing the HTML is one of the highest-return performance optimisations available.` },
      { h: "What about GIF hosting services?", b: `Platforms like Giphy and Tenor actually serve GIFs as video internally — if you embed their player on your site, you're getting an MP4, not a GIF, even though it looks like a GIF. They made the switch years ago because the size difference was too significant to ignore.\n\nFollow the same principle on your own website: convert your GIFs to MP4, use the video element, and let users experience your animations at the speed they were always capable of.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "jpg-to-png-transparency-design", toolId: "jpg-to-png",
    title: "Why Converting JPG to PNG Matters for Designers (And When It Doesn't)",
    date: "February 3, 2026", author: "Leila Nasser", readTime: "4 min read", cat: "Convert",
    excerpt: "The most common reason to convert JPG to PNG is transparency. But there are other situations where the switch makes a real difference — and some where it's pointless.",
    sections: [
      { h: "The transparency problem with JPG", b: `JPG doesn't support transparency. If you place a JPG image on a coloured background, it always has a white or solid background. You can see this immediately when you put a JPG logo on a dark background — the white box around the logo is obvious and unprofessional.\n\nPNG supports an alpha channel that controls each pixel's opacity from fully transparent to fully opaque, letting PNG images blend seamlessly into any background. Converting a JPG to PNG doesn't add transparency — the white background is still there, just in a different format. Removing that background is a separate operation; the conversion is the prerequisite that makes it possible.` },
      { h: "When the conversion makes a real difference", b: `The meaningful use cases are situations where you'll be doing further editing that requires a lossless format. If you have a JPG image and need to edit it, save it, edit it again, and save it again — JPG compression degradation accumulates with each save. Converting to PNG before editing keeps quality perfectly preserved through every edit cycle.\n\nGraphic design work that involves layering, compositing, and many save cycles should happen in PNG, with the final export to JPG happening once at the end.` },
      { h: "When the conversion is pointless", b: `Converting a JPG photo to PNG for the sole purpose of having a PNG file — without any editing, transparency, or quality preservation need — is essentially pointless. The PNG will be 2–5x larger than the JPG, it will look identical, and it will provide no benefit.\n\nMany people do this thinking PNG is inherently better quality than JPG, but for photographs already compressed to JPG, the quality that PNG's lossless compression would preserve is already gone. PNG cannot reconstruct what JPG has already discarded.` },
      { h: "The correct editing workflow", b: `Convert your JPG to PNG at the start. Do all editing in PNG. Export to JPG once at the end, at the quality level appropriate for the intended use.\n\nThis prevents the quality loss that occurs when you open a JPG, edit it, save it as JPG, open that file, edit again, and save again. Each JPG save discards some data. By converting to PNG first, you work in a lossless format throughout the editing process.` },
      { h: "Uploading to websites", b: `Converting JPG to PNG before uploading to a website is counterproductive. PNG photos are significantly larger and slower to load than JPG photos at equivalent quality. Unless the photo requires transparency, keep it as JPG for web use.\n\nThe transparency use case is the main legitimate reason to convert JPG to PNG for web — when you need a photo-like image to have a transparent background for compositing over other content.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "webp-format-explained", toolId: "webp-to-jpg",
    title: "What is WebP and Why Is Your Browser Obsessed With It?",
    date: "January 30, 2026", author: "Tom Everett", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "Convert",
    excerpt: "WebP promises 25–35% smaller file sizes than JPG with equivalent quality. It's supported by every major browser. So why do you keep needing to convert WebP files to JPG?",
    sections: [
      { h: "What Google built and why it matters", b: `WebP was developed by Google and released in 2010, based on the VP8 video codec compression algorithm. Compared to JPEG, WebP typically produces files 25–35% smaller at equivalent quality. Unlike JPEG, WebP supports transparency. Unlike PNG, it can do lossy compression. It also supports animation — what GIF does, but at a fraction of the file size.\n\nIn theory, WebP is the format that does everything better than its predecessors. All major modern browsers now support it natively.` },
      { h: "Why WebP files keep creating problems", b: `All major modern browsers support WebP natively. But outside browsers, WebP support is patchy. Many image editing applications don't open WebP without plugins. Windows Photo Viewer and older Preview versions on Mac don't open WebP. Many upload forms, older CMS platforms, and legacy systems either fail or produce errors with WebP files.\n\nWebP files you download from websites — screenshots captured as WebP, images saved from Chrome — often can't be opened in standard workflows on many computers.` },
      { h: "Converting WebP to JPG: what you lose and keep", b: `Converting WebP to JPG produces a universally compatible file at a modest increase in file size. The visual quality at 85%+ JPG quality is indistinguishable from the WebP original. What you lose is the file size efficiency.\n\nIf the WebP had transparency, converting to JPG loses that — convert to PNG instead if transparency matters. Converting to JPG is the right choice when you need universal compatibility over maximum compression efficiency.` },
      { h: "Should you create WebP files for your website?", b: `If you're building or managing a website and have control over the image pipeline, yes — serving WebP images is worth doing. The file size savings are real, every modern browser supports it, and there are well-established ways to provide JPG fallbacks for older contexts. WordPress now supports WebP natively. Cloudinary and similar services can automatically serve WebP to supporting browsers and JPG to others.\n\nFor web development, WebP is best practice. For general use and sharing, JPG and PNG remain more universally practical.` },
      { h: "The future: AVIF is coming for WebP's crown", b: `Just as WebP displaced older formats, a newer format called AVIF is challenging WebP. AVIF achieves even better compression — typically 50% smaller than JPG — with excellent quality. Browser support is now good across Chrome, Firefox, and Edge, and adoption is growing.\n\nThe practical answer is always to know your output medium: use the best format your delivery context supports, and convert to universally compatible formats when compatibility matters more than efficiency.` }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "remove-background-photography", toolId: "remove-bg",
    title: "Background Removal: When to Use It, When It Backfires, and How to Get Clean Edges",
    date: "January 28, 2026", author: "Sara Okonkwo", readTime: "5 min read", cat: "AI Tools",
    excerpt: "AI background removal has gotten impressively good at product photos. It still struggles with hair, fur, and complex edges. Here's the complete honest guide.",
    sections: [
      { h: "What AI background removal does well", b: `Modern AI background removal excels at subjects with clean, high-contrast edges against simple backgrounds. Product photos are the perfect use case: a shoe against a white studio backdrop, a bag against grey, a bottle against a gradient. The clear boundary between product and background gives the AI plenty of signal to work with, and results are often production-quality without any manual cleanup.\n\nPortraits shot in controlled studio conditions — single subject, plain background, no complex hair or accessories — are another strong suit.` },
      { h: "Where background removal fails", b: `Hair is the classic failure case. Long hair with flyaways, curly hair, braids, and any complex hair shape create detection problems at the edges. The AI has to make pixel-level decisions about whether each area belongs to the subject or the background, and with hair that's partially transparent against a background, those decisions become probabilistic rather than certain.\n\nFur, feathers, and soft textures have the same problem. Glass and transparent objects are another failure mode — the AI doesn't understand transparency and either removes the glass entirely or leaves background showing through incorrectly.` },
      { h: "Getting the best results", b: `Background colour matters enormously. High-contrast backgrounds — a dark subject against white, or a light subject against dark — produce the best results. Low-contrast backgrounds where the subject blends with the background will confuse any AI system.\n\nLighting matters too. Even, flat lighting without strong shadows is easier to process than dramatic lighting that creates dark areas on the subject that might be confused with background.` },
      { h: "Manual refinement after AI removal", b: `For professional applications, AI background removal is the starting point, not the finished product. After the AI does its work, inspect closely and clean up what it got wrong. Common fixes: softening edges that are too hard (looks clipped), removing background halos (faint traces of the original background colour at the edges), and restoring details the AI removed.\n\nThis post-processing step is done in an image editor that supports layers and masks — Photoshop, GIMP, or Affinity Photo. The AI handles 90–95% of the work; manual refinement handles the rest.` },
      { h: "Use cases that actually justify background removal", b: `E-commerce is the highest-value application. A product on a white background sells better than on a cluttered background, studies consistently show. Amazon requires white backgrounds on main product images. AI background removal makes this workflow economically viable for large catalogues.\n\nReal estate photography uses background replacement to show spaces with different styles or swap an overcast sky. Professional headshots get clean, consistent backgrounds. Marketing teams composite product shots into scenes. All legitimate, all valuable when the source image quality supports it.` }
    ]
  }
];
